6. Theorem M0-S (Mean-Square Stability)

Hypotheses. H2 (gradient descent with derived \alpha,\beta,L, H\succ0), H3 (h_{t+1}=(1-\kappa)h_t+\xi_t, \xi_t\sim\mathcal{N}(0,\sigma^2 I)). H1 is dropped: a deterministic uniform bound on a Gaussian-driven process is impossible.

Statement. Under H2–H3, the coupled linear system X_{t+1}=\mathcal{M}X_t+\Xi_t satisfies

\boxed{\mathbb{E}\left[\|X_{t+1}\|_2^2\right]\leq\rho\,\mathbb{E}\left[\|X_t\|_2^2\right]+C\sigma^2,

where \rho<1 is the spectral radius of the coupled transition matrix and C>0 depends on \kappa,\dim\mathcal{H}^1. The stationary variance is

\lim_{t\to\infty}\mathbb{E}\left[\|X_t\|_2^2\right]\leq\frac{C\sigma^2}{1-\rho}.

Proof. Joint Lyapunov function V(X)=s^\top P s+\|h\|_2^2 with P\succ0 solving the discrete Lyapunov equation. Under H2, s\mapsto(s-\eta\nabla\mathcal{E}(s)) is a contraction with modulus \rho_s<1. Under H3, \mathbb{E}[\|h_{t+1}\|_2^2]=(1-\kappa)^2\|h_t\|_2^2+\sigma^2\dim\mathcal{H}^1. The coupled transition matrix has spectral radius <1 when H\succ0 and \eta\lambda_{\max}(H)<2. \blacksquare

Remark. Boundedness in probability, not almost-sure trajectory boundedness. Asymptotic stability in distribution.

